1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,537
Chapter 18: Fetal Disorders.

3
00:00:05,537 --> 00:00:24,562
Fetal Anemia Red Cell Alloimmunization Fetomaternal Hemorrhage Fetal Thrombocytopenia Hydrops Fetalis Mirror Syndrome References Fetal disorders may be acquired—such as alloimmunization; may be genetic —congenital adrenal hyperplasia; or may be sporadic—like many structural malformations.

4
00:00:24,562 --> 00:00:31,212
This chapter reviews fetal anemia and thrombocytopenia and immune and nonimmune fetal hydrops.

5
00:00:31,212 --> 00:00:39,975
Hydrops is perhaps the quintessential fetal disorder, as it can be a manifestation of severe illness from a wide variety of etiologies.

6
00:00:39,975 --> 00:00:52,800
Fetal structural malformations are reviewed in Chapter 15, genetic abnormalities in Chapters 16 and 17, and conditions amenable to medical or surgical fetal therapy in Chapter 19.

7
00:00:52,800 --> 00:01:02,125
Because congenital infections arise as a result of maternal infection or colonization, they are discussed in Chapters 67 and 68.

8
00:01:02,125 --> 00:01:04,025
Fetal Anemia.

9
00:01:04,025 --> 00:01:11,075
Anemia may result from alloimmunization, infection, genetic disorders, or fetomaternal hemorrhage.

10
00:01:11,075 --> 00:01:18,712
Red cell alloimmunization results from transplacental passage of maternal antibodies that destroy fetal red cells.

11
00:01:18,712 --> 00:01:31,662
Alloimmunization leads to overproduction of immature fetal and neonatal red cells—erythroblastosis fetalis—a condition now referred to as hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn (HDFN).

12
00:01:31,662 --> 00:01:42,400
Congenital infections are also associated with fetal anemia, particularly parvovirus B19, discussed in Chapter 67 (p. 1191).

13
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:50,562
In Southeast Asian populations, α0-thalassemia is a common cause of severe anemia and nonimmune hydrops.

14
00:01:50,562 --> 00:02:20,612
Rare genetic causes of anemia include red cell production disorders—such as DiamondBlackfan anemia and Fanconi anemia; red cell enzymopathies—glucose-6phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency and pyruvate kinase deficiency; red cell structural abnormalities—hereditary spherocytosis and elliptocytosis; lysosomal storage diseases—Gaucher disease, Niemann-Pick, and mucopolysaccharidosis VII; and myeloproliferative disorders—leukemias.

15
00:02:20,612 --> 00:02:25,312
Fetomaternal hemorrhage is discussed on page 357.

16
00:02:25,312 --> 00:02:36,187
Fetal anemia is typically identified through Doppler evaluation of the fetal middle cerebral artery (MCA) peak systolic velocity (p. 355).

17
00:02:36,187 --> 00:02:43,137
Progressive fetal anemia from any cause leads to heart failure, hydrops fetalis, and ultimately death.

18
00:02:43,137 --> 00:02:47,300
Treatment with intrauterine transfusions can be lifesaving.

19
00:02:47,300 --> 00:03:04,925
Severely anemic fetuses transfused in utero have survival rates exceeding 90 percent, and even in cases of hydrops fetalis, survival rates approach 80 percent (Lindenberg, 2013; Mizuuchi, 2021; Zwiers, 2017).

20
00:03:04,925 --> 00:03:08,125
# Red Cell Alloimmunization.

21
00:03:08,125 --> 00:03:18,250
Currently, 36 different blood group systems and 360 erythrocyte antigens are recognized by the International Society of Blood Transfusion .

22
00:03:18,250 --> 00:03:22,350
Blood banks routinely screen for erythrocyte antigens.

23
00:03:22,350 --> 00:03:29,012
Some are immunologically and genetically important, but many are rare and of little clinical significance.

24
00:03:29,012 --> 00:03:36,500
An individual who lacks a specific erythrocyte antigen may produce antibodies against it when exposed to that antigen.

25
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:42,762
Such antibodies can prove harmful if an individual receives an incompatible blood transfusion.

26
00:03:42,762 --> 00:03:52,262
During pregnancy, these antibodies may cross the placenta and lyse fetal red cells that contain the associated antigens, resulting in anemia.

27
00:03:52,262 --> 00:03:58,550
The fetus typically inherits at least one erythrocyte antigen from the father that is lacking in the mother.

28
00:03:58,550 --> 00:04:06,025
The pregnant woman may become sensitized if enough fetal red cells reach her circulation to elicit an immune response.

29
00:04:06,025 --> 00:04:34,700
Importantly, alloimmunization is uncommon for the following reasons: (1) low prevalence of incompatible erythrocyte antigens; (2) insufficient transplacental passage of fetal antigens and maternal antibodies; (3) maternal-fetal ABO incompatibility, which leads to rapid clearance of fetal erythrocytes before they elicit an immune response; (4) variable antigenicity; and (5) variable maternal immune response to the antigen.

30
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:44,425
The prevalence of red cell alloimmunization in pregnancy approximates 1 percent (Bollason, 2017; Koelewijn, 2008).

31
00:04:44,425 --> 00:04:51,237
Most cases of severe fetal anemia requiring antenatal transfusion are attributable to anti-D,

32
00:04:51,388 --> 00:04:58,025
anti-Kell, anti-c, or anti-E alloimmunization (de Haas, 2015).

33
00:04:57,975 --> 00:05:03,888
Maternal blood type and antibody screen are routinely assessed at the first prenatal visit.

34
00:05:03,888 --> 00:05:09,350
Unbound antibodies in maternal serum are detected with an indirect Coombs test .

35
00:05:09,350 --> 00:05:22,600
If the result is positive, the specific antibodies are identified; their immunoglobulin subtype is determined as either immunoglobulin G (IgG) or M (IgM); and the titer is quantified.

36
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:29,275
Only IgG antibodies are concerning, because IgM antibodies do not cross the placenta.

37
00:05:29,275 --> 00:05:36,400
Selected antibodies and their potential to cause fetal hemolytic anemia are listed in Table 18-1.

38
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:41,513
The critical titer is the level at which significant fetal anemia may develop.

39
00:05:41,513 --> 00:05:48,288
It may vary according to antibody and laboratory but is usually between 1:8 and 1:32.

40
00:05:48,288 --> 00:06:00,225
If the laboratory’s critical titer threshold for anti-D antibodies is 1:16, a titer ≥1:16 indicates the possibility of severe hemolytic disease.

41
00:06:00,225 --> 00:06:06,650
An important exception is Kell sensitization, which is discussed on page 354.

42
00:06:06,650 --> 00:06:09,113
Table 18-1.

43
00:06:09,113 --> 00:06:28,938
Selected Red Cell Antigens and Their Relationship to Fetal Hemolytic Disease CDE (Rh) Blood Group Incompatibility The CDE system includes five erythrocyte antigens: C, c, D, E, and e. There is no “d” antigen, and D-negativity is defined as the absence of the D antigen.

44
00:06:28,938 --> 00:06:35,213
Although most people are D positive or negative, more than 200 D antigen variants exist .

45
00:06:35,213 --> 00:06:44,463
The CDE group was formerly termed Rh or rhesus, due to a misconception that red cells from rhesus monkeys expressed these human antigens.

46
00:06:44,463 --> 00:06:48,638
In transfusion medicine, “rhesus” is no longer used .

47
00:06:48,638 --> 00:06:52,050
CDE antigens are clinically important.

48
00:06:52,050 --> 00:07:00,850
D-negative individuals may become sensitized after a single exposure to as little as 0.1 mL of fetal erythrocytes .

49
00:07:00,850 --> 00:07:11,913
The two responsible genes—RHD and RHCE —are located on the short arm of chromosome 1 and are inherited together, independent of other blood group genes.

50
00:07:11,913 --> 00:07:16,388
Antigen positivity varies according to race and ethnicity.

51
00:07:16,388 --> 00:07:31,450
Nearly 85 percent of non-Hispanic white Americans are D-positive, as are approximately 90 percent of Native Americans, 93 percent of African Americans and Hispanic Americans, and 99 percent of Asian Americans .

52
00:07:31,450 --> 00:07:42,625
The prevalence of D alloimmunization complicating pregnancy ranges from 0.5 to 0.9 percent (Koelewijn, 2008; Martin, 2005).

53
00:07:42,625 --> 00:07:54,338
Without anti-D immune globulin prophylaxis, a D-negative woman delivered of a Dpositive, ABO-compatible newborn has a 16-percent likelihood of developing alloimmunization.

54
00:07:54,338 --> 00:08:07,975
Two percent will become sensitized by the time of delivery, 7 percent by 6 months postpartum, and the remaining 7 percent will be “sensibilized”—producing detectable antibodies only in a subsequent pregnancy .

55
00:08:07,975 --> 00:08:18,625
If there is ABO incompatibility, the D alloimmunization risk decreases to 2 percent because erythrocyte destruction of incompatible cells limits sensitization .

56
00:08:18,625 --> 00:08:27,925
D sensitization may also occur following first-trimester pregnancy complications, prenatal diagnostic procedures, and maternal trauma .

57
00:08:27,925 --> 00:08:30,400
Table 18-2.

58
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:43,113
Causes of Fetomaternal Hemorrhage Associated with Red Cell Antigen Alloimmunizationa The C, c, E, and e antigens have lower immunogenicity than the D antigen but can cause hemolytic disease.

59
00:08:43,113 --> 00:08:59,900
Sensitization to E, c, and C antigens complicates approximately 0.3 percent of pregnancies in screening studies and accounts for about 30 percent of red cell alloimmunization cases (Howard, 1998; Koelewijn, 2008).

60
00:08:59,900 --> 00:09:16,700
Anti-E alloimmunization is the most common, but the need for fetal or neonatal transfusions is greater with anti-c alloimmunization than with anti-E or anti-C (de Haas, 2015; Hackney, 2004; Koelewijn, 2008).

61
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:18,838
The Grandmother Effect.

62
00:09:18,838 --> 00:09:24,675
In virtually all pregnancies, small amounts of maternal blood enter the fetal circulation.

63
00:09:24,675 --> 00:09:34,713
Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) has identified maternal D-positive DNA in peripheral blood from preterm and full-term D-negative newborns .

64
00:09:34,713 --> 00:09:46,338
Thus, a D-negative female fetus exposed to maternal D-positive red cells might develop sensitization, and later might produce anti-D antibodies before or during pregnancy.

65
00:09:46,338 --> 00:09:51,113
This mechanism is called the grandmother effect because the fetus in the current

66
00:09:51,268 --> 00:09:58,643
pregnancy is jeopardized by maternal antibodies that were initially provoked by his or her grandmother’s erythrocytes.

67
00:09:58,593 --> 00:10:19,418
Alloimmunization to Minor Antigens Because routine administration of anti-D immune globulin prevents anti-D alloimmunization, proportionately more cases of hemolytic disease are caused by red cell antigens other than D (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019a; Koelewijn, 2008).

68
00:10:19,418 --> 00:10:22,593
These are also known as minor antigens.

69
00:10:22,593 --> 00:10:25,730
Kell antigens are among the most frequent.

70
00:10:25,730 --> 00:10:38,193
Other antigens with potential to cause severe alloimmunization include Duffy group A—Fya, MNS, and Kidd—Jka (de Hass, 2015; Moise, 2008).

71
00:10:38,193 --> 00:10:44,980
In most cases, sensitization to a minor antigen results from an incompatible blood transfusion.

72
00:10:44,980 --> 00:10:55,130
However, if an IgG red cell antibody is detected and there is any doubt as to its significance, the pregnancy should be evaluated for hemolytic disease.

73
00:10:55,130 --> 00:10:59,080
Only a few blood group antigens pose no fetal risk.

74
00:10:59,080 --> 00:11:04,818
Lewis antibodies— Lea and Leb—are cold agglutinins, as are I antibodies.

75
00:11:04,818 --> 00:11:09,730
They are predominantly IgM and are not expressed on fetal red cells .

76
00:11:09,730 --> 00:11:15,668
Another antibody that does not cause fetal hemolysis is Duffy group B—Fyb.

77
00:11:15,668 --> 00:11:18,030
Kell Alloimmunization.

78
00:11:18,030 --> 00:11:26,730
Approximately 90 percent of non-Hispanic white Americans and up to 98 percent of African Americans are Kell antigen negative.

79
00:11:26,730 --> 00:11:29,718
Kell type is not routinely determined.

80
00:11:29,718 --> 00:11:38,543
Transfusion history is important, as nearly 90 percent of Kell sensitization cases result from transfusion with Kell-positive blood.

81
00:11:38,543 --> 00:11:46,555
Kell sensitization may develop more rapidly and may be more severe than with sensitization to D and other blood group antigens.

82
00:11:46,555 --> 00:11:56,080
This is because Kell antibodies attach to erythrocyte precursors in the fetal bone marrow and thereby impair the normal hemopoietic response to anemia.

83
00:11:56,080 --> 00:12:05,093
With fewer erythrocytes produced, there is less hemolysis, and thus severe anemia may not be predicted by the maternal Kell antibody titer.

84
00:12:05,093 --> 00:12:14,655
Slootweg and colleagues (2018) reviewed 93 pregnancies with Kell alloimmunization in which the fetus was confirmed to be Kell-positive.

85
00:12:14,655 --> 00:12:28,268
They found that a titer of 1:4 had 100 percent sensitivity, 27 percent specificity, and 60 percent positive predictive value for transfusion requirement in the fetal or neonatal period.

86
00:12:28,268 --> 00:12:34,580
More than 50 percent of Kell antigen–positive fetuses ultimately needed transfusions.

87
00:12:34,580 --> 00:12:48,043
Given the potential for severe anemia, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) has recommended that antibody titers not be used to monitor Kell-sensitized pregnancies.

88
00:12:48,043 --> 00:13:00,980
ABO Blood Group Incompatibility Incompatibility for the major blood group antigens A and B is the most common cause of hemolytic disease in newborns, but it does not cause appreciable hemolysis in the fetus.

89
00:13:00,980 --> 00:13:08,043
This is because most anti-A and anti-B antibodies are IgM types and do not cross the placenta.

90
00:13:08,043 --> 00:13:15,580
Also, fetal red cells have fewer A and B antigenic sites than adult cells and are thus less immunogenic.

91
00:13:15,580 --> 00:13:23,880
Approximately 20 percent of newborns have ABO blood group incompatibility, although only 5 percent are affected clinically.

92
00:13:23,880 --> 00:13:28,418
In such cases, the resulting anemia is typically mild.

93
00:13:28,418 --> 00:13:33,568
The condition differs from CDE incompatibility in several respects.

94
00:13:33,568 --> 00:13:41,893
First, ABO incompatibility is often seen in firstborn neonates, unlike sensitization to other blood group antigens.

95
00:13:41,893 --> 00:13:52,230
This is because most group O women have developed anti-A and anti-B isoagglutinins before pregnancy from exposure to bacteria displaying similar antigens.

96
00:13:52,230 --> 00:13:58,768
Additionally, ABO alloimmunization rarely becomes more severe in successive pregnancies.

97
00:13:58,768 --> 00:14:05,693
Fetal surveillance and early delivery are not indicated in pregnancies with prior ABO incompatibility.

98
00:14:05,693 --> 00:14:15,043
Postnatally however, neonates are evaluated for hyperbilirubinemia, which may require treatment with phototherapy or occasionally transfusion .

99
00:14:15,043 --> 00:14:18,918
# Management of the Alloimmunized Pregnancy.

100
00:14:18,918 --> 00:14:31,018
Of fetuses from D-alloimmunized pregnancies, 25 to 30 percent will have mild to moderate hemolytic anemia, and up to 25 percent have anemia severe enough to cause hydrops .

101
00:14:31,018 --> 00:14:37,430
If alloimmunization is detected and the titer is below the critical value, the titer is generally

102
00:14:37,588 --> 00:14:41,638
repeated every 4 weeks for the duration of the pregnancy .

103
00:14:41,588 --> 00:14:49,075
In any pregnancy in which the antibody titer has reached a critical value, there is no benefit to repeating the titer.

104
00:14:49,075 --> 00:14:55,038
The pregnancy is at risk even if the titer drops, and further evaluation is required.

105
00:14:55,038 --> 00:15:03,313
Similarly, if a prior pregnancy was complicated by alloimmunization, the pregnancy is considered at risk regardless of titer.

106
00:15:03,313 --> 00:15:13,050
Fetal Risk Assessment The presence of anti-D antibodies reflects maternal sensitization but does not indicate whether the fetus is D-positive or D-negative.

107
00:15:13,050 --> 00:15:18,300
Up to 40 percent of D-negative pregnant women carry a D-negative fetus.

108
00:15:18,300 --> 00:15:29,238
If a woman is sensitized from a prior pregnancy, her antibody titer may rise during the current pregnancy even if the current fetus is D-negative because of an amnestic response.

109
00:15:29,238 --> 00:15:36,850
In a non-Hispanic white couple in which the woman is D-negative, there is an 85-percent chance that the man is D-positive.

110
00:15:36,850 --> 00:15:46,025
However, there is a 60-percent likelihood that he is heterozygous at the Dlocus, and only half of his children will be at risk for hemolytic disease.

111
00:15:46,025 --> 00:15:53,088
Initial evaluation of alloimmunization begins with determining the paternal erythrocyte antigen status.

112
00:15:53,088 --> 00:16:02,175
Provided that paternity is certain, if the father is negative for the red cell antigen to which the mother is sensitized, the pregnancy is not at risk.

113
00:16:02,175 --> 00:16:08,713
A prior blood transfusion may be the cause of alloimmunization to a red cell antigen other than D.

114
00:16:08,713 --> 00:16:18,825
In a D-alloimmunized pregnancy in which the father is D-positive, it is helpful to determine paternal zygosity for the D antigen using DNA-based analysis.

115
00:16:18,825 --> 00:16:26,375
If the father is heterozygous—or if paternity is not known—the woman should be offered assessment of fetal genotype.

116
00:16:26,375 --> 00:16:40,075
Traditionally, this was done with amniocentesis and PCR testing of uncultured amniocytes, which has a positive predictive value of 100 percent and negative predictive value of approximately 97 percent .

117
00:16:40,075 --> 00:16:50,288
Fetal testing for other antigens—such as E/e, C/c, Duffy, Kell, Kidd, and M/N—also is available with this method.

118
00:16:50,288 --> 00:16:58,850
Chorionic villus sampling is not recommended because of greater risk for fetomaternal hemorrhage and subsequent worsening of alloimmunization .

119
00:16:58,850 --> 00:17:07,038
Noninvasive fetal D genotyping has been performed using cell-free DNA (cfDNA) from maternal plasma .

120
00:17:07,038 --> 00:17:23,950
The reported sensitivity exceeds 99 percent, the specificity exceeds 95 percent, and positive or negative predictive values are similarly very high (Johnson, 2017; Moise, 2016; Pazourkova, 2021).

121
00:17:23,950 --> 00:17:32,763
Fetal D genotyping with cfDNA is routinely used in parts of Europe but is not yet a widely used clinical tool in the United States .

122
00:17:32,763 --> 00:17:54,225
Two potential indications for cfDNA use in D-negative pregnant women are: (1) in the setting of D alloimmunization, testing can identify fetuses who are also D-negative and do not require anemia surveillance, and (2) in women without D alloimmunization, anti-D immune globulin might be withheld if the fetus is D negative.

123
00:17:54,225 --> 00:18:08,250
In the case of the latter, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) does not recommend routine cfDNA screening in D-negative pregnancies until it has been demonstrated to be cost-effective.

124
00:18:08,250 --> 00:18:21,613
Management of the alloimmunized pregnancy typically consists of maternal antibody titer surveillance followed by ultrasound monitoring of the fetal MCA peak systolic velocity if a critical antibody titer is reached.

125
00:18:21,613 --> 00:18:30,913
As noted earlier (p. 354), pregnancies with Kell alloimmunization often receive ultrasound surveillance regardless of titer.

126
00:18:30,913 --> 00:18:42,488
Fetal blood sampling is generally performed if the MCA peak systolic velocity exceeds the threshold for severe anemia, with plan for concurrent intrauterine transfusion as needed.

127
00:18:42,488 --> 00:18:51,675
Spectrophotometric analysis of amnionic fluid bilirubin, also known as the ΔOD450 test, is no longer recommended .

128
00:18:51,675 --> 00:19:04,400
Recent efforts have focused on use of maternal intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) therapy to postpone the initial intrauterine transfusion to beyond 20 weeks in severely affected pregnancies .

129
00:19:04,400 --> 00:19:13,725
Its mechanism of action is unclear, but IVIG therapy has been reported to delay need for transfusion by an average of 3 weeks and to

130
00:19:13,876 --> 00:19:16,463
lower the risk for hydrops .

131
00:19:16,413 --> 00:19:19,888
Middle Cerebral Artery Doppler Velocimetry.

132
00:19:19,888 --> 00:19:27,526
Serial measurement of the peak systolic velocity of the fetal MCA is the recommended test to detect fetal anemia .

133
00:19:27,526 --> 00:19:33,526
The anemic fetus shunts blood preferentially to the brain to maintain adequate oxygenation.

134
00:19:33,526 --> 00:19:39,438
The velocity rises because of increased cardiac output and decreased blood viscosity.

135
00:19:39,438 --> 00:19:44,738
Measurement technique is discussed in Chapter 14 (p. 262).

136
00:19:44,738 --> 00:19:58,638
In a landmark study, Mari and coworkers (2000) measured the MCA peak systolic velocity serially in 111 fetuses at risk for anemia and in 265 normal control fetuses.

137
00:19:58,638 --> 00:20:08,613
The threshold value of 1.5 multiples of the median (MoM) for gestational age correctly identified all fetuses with moderate or severe anemia.

138
00:20:08,613 --> 00:20:14,838
This provided a sensitivity of 100 percent, with a falsepositive rate of 12 percent.

139
00:20:14,838 --> 00:20:23,463
MCA peak systolic velocity is followed serially, and values are plotted on a curve like the one shown in Figure 18-1.

140
00:20:23,463 --> 00:20:36,288
If the velocity is between 1.0 and 1.5 MoM and the slope is rising—such that the value is approaching 1.5 MoM—MCA Doppler surveillance is generally performed at least weekly.

141
00:20:36,288 --> 00:20:52,538
The false-positive rate of MCA peak systolic velocity increases significantly beyond 34 weeks’ gestation and stems from the normal rise in cardiac output that develops at this gestational age (Moise, 2008; Zimmerman, 2002).

142
00:20:52,538 --> 00:21:03,038
At Parkland Hospital, MCA peak systolic velocity is not measured beyond 35 weeks, but ultrasound evaluation for hydrops is performed as needed.

143
00:21:03,038 --> 00:21:05,513
Figure 18-1.

144
00:21:05,513 --> 00:21:15,538
Doppler measurements of the peak systolic velocity in the middle cerebral artery (MCA) in 165 fetuses at risk for severe anemia.

145
00:21:15,538 --> 00:21:24,626
The blue line indicates the median peak systolic velocity in normal pregnancies, and the red line shows 1.5 multiples of the median.

146
00:21:24,626 --> 00:21:42,688
Reproduced with permission from Oepkes D, Seaward PG, Vandenbussche et al: Doppler ultrasonography versus amniocentesis to predict fetal anemia, N Engl J Med. 2006 Jul 13;355(2):156–164.

147
00:21:42,688 --> 00:21:56,613
Fetal Blood Transfusion If the MCA peak systolic velocity exceeds 1.5 MoM or if hydrops develops and anemia is the leading etiology, fetal blood sampling and intrauterine transfusion should be considered.

148
00:21:56,613 --> 00:22:02,726
Fetal transfusion is typically performed prior to 34 to 35 weeks’ gestation .

149
00:22:02,726 --> 00:22:09,426
Later in gestation, the benefits of transfusion may be outweighed by the risks of delaying delivery.

150
00:22:09,426 --> 00:22:12,963
Transfusion is most commonly intravascular.

151
00:22:12,963 --> 00:22:24,713
However, the umbilical vein may be too narrow in the early second trimester to readily permit needle entry, and severe hemolysis may necessitate intraperitoneal fetal transfusion.

152
00:22:24,713 --> 00:22:34,763
In the setting of hydrops, peritoneal absorption may be impaired, and some prefer to transfuse into both the fetal peritoneal cavity and umbilical vein.

153
00:22:34,763 --> 00:22:40,851
Transfusion is generally recommended only if the fetal hematocrit is <30 percent .

154
00:22:40,851 --> 00:22:46,288
If hydrops has developed, the hematocrit is usually 15 percent or lower.

155
00:22:46,288 --> 00:23:01,538
The red cells transfused are type O, D-negative, cytomegalovirus-negative, packed to a hematocrit of approximately 80 percent to prevent volume overload, irradiated to prevent fetal graft-versus-host reaction, and leukocyte-poor.

156
00:23:01,538 --> 00:23:07,726
The fetal–placental volume allows rapid infusion of a relatively large quantity of blood.

157
00:23:07,726 --> 00:23:14,626
Before transfusion, a paralytic agent such as vecuronium may be given to the fetus to minimize movement.

158
00:23:14,626 --> 00:23:19,976
In a nonhydropic fetus, the target hematocrit is 40 to 50 percent.

159
00:23:19,976 --> 00:23:30,226
The volume transfused may be estimated by multiplying the estimated fetal weight in grams by 0.02 for each 10-percent rise in hematocrit needed .

160
00:23:30,226 --> 00:23:41,913
In the severely anemic fetus at 18 to 24 weeks’ gestation, a smaller volume is transfused initially, and another transfusion may be planned for approximately 2 days later.

161
00:23:41,913 --> 00:23:47,888
Subsequent transfusions take place every 2 to 4 weeks, depending on the hematocrit.

162
00:23:47,888 --> 00:23:59,163
The MCA peak systolic velocity threshold for severe anemia is higher following an initial transfusion—1.70 MoM rather than 1.50 MoM .

163
00:23:59,163 --> 00:24:09,976
It is hypothesized that the change in threshold compensates for the contribution of donor cells in the initial transfusion, because the donor cells are from adults and have a smaller mean

164
00:24:10,132 --> 00:24:12,319
corpuscular volume.

165
00:24:12,269 --> 00:24:18,544
Following transfusion, the fetal hematocrit drops by approximately 1 percent per day.

166
00:24:18,544 --> 00:24:22,919
The initial decline may be more rapid if hydrops has developed.

167
00:24:22,919 --> 00:24:24,607
Outcomes.

168
00:24:24,607 --> 00:24:32,894
The overall survival rate approximates 95 percent (Zwiers, 2017; Mizuuchi, 2021).

169
00:24:32,894 --> 00:24:45,557
Complications include fetal death in 2 percent, need for emergent cesarean delivery in 1 percent, and infection and preterm rupture of membranes in 0.3 percent each, respectively.

170
00:24:45,557 --> 00:24:55,694
The stillbirth rate exceeds 15 percent if transfusion is required before 20 weeks (Lindenberg, 2013; Zwiers, 2017).

171
00:24:55,694 --> 00:25:04,669
For hydropic fetuses, the neonatal survival rate is about 80 percent (Emiroglu, 2020; Van Kamp, 2001).

172
00:25:04,669 --> 00:25:15,432
In one series, 95 percent of neonates survived if hydrops resolved following intrauterine transfusion compared with fewer than 40 percent if hydrops persisted .

173
00:25:15,432 --> 00:25:25,832
Lindenberg (2012) reviewed long-term outcomes following intrauterine transfusion in a cohort of more than 450 alloimmunized pregnancies.

174
00:25:25,832 --> 00:25:33,944
Alloimmunization was secondary to anti-D in 80 percent, anti-Kell in 12 percent, and anti-c in 5 percent.

175
00:25:33,944 --> 00:25:42,857
Approximately a fourth of affected fetuses had hydrops, and more than half also required exchange transfusion in the neonatal period.

176
00:25:42,857 --> 00:25:52,607
Among nearly 300 children aged 2 to 17 years who participated in neurodevelopmental testing, fewer than 5 percent had severe impairments.

177
00:25:52,607 --> 00:25:56,419
# Prevention of Anti-D Alloimmunization.

178
00:25:56,419 --> 00:26:03,032
Anti-D immune globulin has been used for more than five decades to prevent D alloimmunization.

179
00:26:03,032 --> 00:26:10,607
Unfortunately, 50 percent of women around the world who would benefit from anti-D immune globulin do not receive it .

180
00:26:10,607 --> 00:26:19,119
In countries without access, up to 10 percent of D-negative pregnancies are complicated by hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn .

181
00:26:19,119 --> 00:26:26,694
With immunoprophylaxis, however, the alloimmunization risk may be reduced to <0.2 percent.

182
00:26:26,694 --> 00:26:33,082
Despite long-standing and widespread use, its mechanism of action is not completely understood.

183
00:26:33,082 --> 00:26:39,732
Fetomaternal hemorrhage at delivery accounts for as many as 90 percent of alloimmunization cases.

184
00:26:39,732 --> 00:26:50,557
Routine postpartum administration of anti-D immune globulin to at-risk pregnancies within 72 hours of delivery lowers the alloimmunization rate by 90 percent .

185
00:26:50,557 --> 00:27:02,244
Additionally, provision of anti-D immune globulin at 28 weeks’ gestation reduces the third-trimester alloimmunization rate from approximately 2 percent to 0.1 percent .

186
00:27:02,244 --> 00:27:07,432
Whenever there is doubt whether to give anti-D immune globulin, it should be given.

187
00:27:07,432 --> 00:27:14,294
If not needed, it will not cause harm, but failure to provide it when needed may have severe consequences.

188
00:27:14,294 --> 00:27:23,319
Anti-D immune globulin is derived from human plasma donated by individuals with high-titer anti-D immunoglobulin D antibodies.

189
00:27:23,319 --> 00:27:35,794
Formulations prepared by cold ethanol fractionation and ultrafiltration must be administered intramuscularly because they contain plasma proteins that could result in anaphylaxis if given intravenously.

190
00:27:35,794 --> 00:27:47,282
Formulations prepared using ion exchange chromatography may be administered either intramuscularly or intravenously, which is relevant when treating significant fetomaternal hemorrhage.

191
00:27:47,282 --> 00:27:55,557
Both preparation methods effectively remove viral particles, including hepatitis and human immunodeficiency viruses.

192
00:27:55,557 --> 00:28:06,982
Depending on the preparation, the half-life of anti-D immune globulin ranges from 16 to 24 days, which is why it is given both in the third trimester and following delivery.

193
00:28:06,982 --> 00:28:22,782
The standard intramuscular dose of anti-D immune globulin—300 μg or 1500 IU—will protect the average-sized mother from a fetal hemorrhage of up to 30 mL of fetal whole blood or 15 mL of fetal red cells.

194
00:28:22,782 --> 00:28:37,194
In the United States, anti-D immune globulin is given prophylactically to all D-negative, unsensitized women at approximately 28 weeks’ gestation, and a second dose is given after delivery if the newborn is D-positive .

195
00:28:37,194 --> 00:28:47,057
Before the 28-week dose of anti-D immune globulin, repeat antibody screening is recommended to identify individuals who have become alloimmunized .

196
00:28:47,057 --> 00:28:52,969
Following delivery, anti-D immune globulin should be given within 72 hours.

197
00:28:52,969 --> 00:28:58,982
Recognizing that 40 percent of neonates born to D-negative women are also D negative,

198
00:28:59,140 --> 00:29:05,840
administration of immune globulin is recommended only after the newborn is confirmed to be D positive .

199
00:29:05,790 --> 00:29:17,452
If immune globulin is inadvertently not administered following delivery, it should be given as soon as the omission is recognized, because there may be some protection up to 28 days postpartum .

200
00:29:17,452 --> 00:29:24,765
Anti-D immune globulin is also administered after pregnancy-related events that could result in sensitization .

201
00:29:24,765 --> 00:29:36,015
In the first trimester, smaller doses of 50 or 120 µg may be suitable, as discussed in Chapters 11 and 12 (p. 203 and 221).

202
00:29:36,015 --> 00:29:44,527
Anti-D immune globulin may produce a weakly positive—1:1 to 1:4— indirect Coombs titer in the mother.

203
00:29:44,527 --> 00:29:49,652
This is harmless and should not be confused with development of alloimmunization.

204
00:29:49,652 --> 00:30:05,640
Additionally, as the body mass index increases above 27 to 40 kg/m2, serum antibody levels decrease by 30 to 60 percent and may be less protective (MacKenzie, 2006; Woelfer, 2004).

205
00:30:05,640 --> 00:30:18,815
D-negative women who receive other types of blood products— including platelet transfusions and plasmapheresis—also are at risk of becoming sensitized, and this can be prevented with anti-D immune globulin.

206
00:30:18,815 --> 00:30:27,565
Rarely, a small amount of antibody crosses the placenta and results in a weakly positive direct Coombs test in cord and infant blood.

207
00:30:27,565 --> 00:30:34,165
Despite this, passive immunization does not cause significant fetal or neonatal hemolysis.

208
00:30:34,165 --> 00:30:42,102
In 2 to 3 per 1000 pregnancies, the volume of fetomaternal hemorrhage may exceed 30 mL of whole blood .

209
00:30:42,102 --> 00:30:47,840
A single dose of anti-D immune globulin would be insufficient in such situations.

210
00:30:47,840 --> 00:30:59,640
If additional anti-D immune globulin is considered only for women with risk factors such as those shown in Table 18-2, half of those who require additional immune globulin may be missed.

211
00:30:59,640 --> 00:31:08,740
For this reason, all D-negative women should be screened at delivery, typically with a rosette test, followed by quantitative testing if indicated .

212
00:31:08,740 --> 00:31:17,077
The rosette test is a qualitative test that identifies whether fetal Dpositive cells are present in the circulation of a D-negative woman.

213
00:31:17,077 --> 00:31:24,402
A sample of maternal blood is mixed with anti-D antibodies that coat any Dpositive fetal cells present in the sample.

214
00:31:24,402 --> 00:31:33,727
Indicator red cells bearing the Dantigen are then added, and rosettes form around the fetal cells as the indicator cells attach to them by the antibodies.

215
00:31:33,727 --> 00:31:39,315
Thus, if rosettes are visualized, there are fetal D-positive cells in that sample.

216
00:31:39,315 --> 00:31:51,552
In the setting of D incompatibility, or any time a large fetomaternal hemorrhage is suspected— regardless of antigen status, a Kleihauer-Betke test or flow cytometry test are used.

217
00:31:51,552 --> 00:31:55,152
These are discussed on page 358.

218
00:31:55,152 --> 00:32:05,077
The dosage of anti-D immune globulin is calculated from the estimated volume of the fetal-to-maternal hemorrhage, as described on page 358.

219
00:32:05,077 --> 00:32:14,165
One 300-μg dose is given for each 15 mL of fetal red cells or 30 mL of fetal whole blood to be neutralized.

220
00:32:14,165 --> 00:32:22,915
If using an intramuscular preparation of antiD immune globulin, no more than five doses may be given in a 24-hour period.

221
00:32:22,915 --> 00:32:31,102
If using an intravenous preparation, two ampules—totaling 600 μg— may be given every 8 hours.

222
00:32:31,102 --> 00:32:39,165
A positive indirect Coombs test or the presence of circulating fetal cells on a rosette test demonstrate that the dose was sufficient.

223
00:32:39,165 --> 00:32:47,727
Serological Weak D Phenotypes Formerly called Du, these are the most common antigenic D variants in the United States and Europe .

224
00:32:47,727 --> 00:32:57,002
Serological weak D phenotypes have been further refined into two general categories using molecular analysis to complete RHD genotyping.

225
00:32:57,002 --> 00:33:03,677
Molecular weak D phenotypes carry reduced numbers of intact D antigens on the red cell surface.

226
00:33:03,677 --> 00:33:10,027
Partial D types have protein deletions associated with abnormal D antigens that lack epitopes .

227
00:33:10,027 --> 00:33:16,577
Many individuals who test positive for weak D have weak D phenotypes 1, 2, or 3.

228
00:33:16,577 --> 00:33:20,377
These phenotypes may be managed as if D-positive.

229
00:33:20,377 --> 00:33:31,052
The pregnancy is not considered at risk for alloimmunization, and thus anti-D immune globulin is not needed (Sandler 2015, 2017).

230
00:33:31,052 --> 00:33:39,115
In contrast, individuals with partial D antigens may be at risk for D-sensitization and do require immune globulin.

231
00:33:39,115 --> 00:33:44,890
Molecular RHD genotyping has been suggested for pregnant women with weak D

232
00:33:45,052 --> 00:33:50,452
phenotype, but cost-benefit analysis of this strategy is presently lacking .

233
00:33:50,402 --> 00:33:59,314
If molecular genetic testing has not been performed in those with serologic weak D phenotype, D immunoprophylaxis should be administered.

234
00:33:59,314 --> 00:34:01,677
Fetomaternal Hemorrhage.

235
00:34:01,677 --> 00:34:10,864
A small amount of fetomaternal bleeding likely occurs in all pregnancies and may be sufficient to provoke an antigen-antibody reaction in two thirds.

236
00:34:10,864 --> 00:34:20,064
As shown in Figure 18-2, the incidence increases with advancing gestation and with the volume of fetal blood in the maternal circulation .

237
00:34:20,064 --> 00:34:27,914
The prevalence of fetomaternal bleeding of at least 30 mL is estimated to be 3 events per 1000 pregnancies .

238
00:34:27,914 --> 00:34:31,102
Fortunately, frank hemorrhage is rare.

239
00:34:31,102 --> 00:34:44,252
In one series of more than 30,000 pregnancies, fetomaternal hemorrhage ≥150 mL complicated 1 in 2800 births (de Almeida, 1994).

240
00:34:44,252 --> 00:34:46,664
Figure 18-2.

241
00:34:46,664 --> 00:34:50,527
Incidence of fetomaternal hemorrhage during pregnancy.

242
00:34:50,527 --> 00:34:58,339
The numbers at each data point represent total volume of fetal blood estimated to have been transferred into the maternal circulation.

243
00:34:58,339 --> 00:35:03,702
Selected causes of fetomaternal hemorrhage are shown in Table 18-2.

244
00:35:03,702 --> 00:35:13,152
With significant hemorrhage, the most common presenting complaint is decreased fetal movement (Bellussi, 2017; Wylie, 2010).

245
00:35:13,152 --> 00:35:19,327
A sinusoidal fetal heart rate pattern is infrequently seen but warrants immediate evaluation .

246
00:35:19,327 --> 00:35:31,877
Sonography may demonstrate elevated MCA peak systolic velocity, and indeed this is reported to be the most accurate predictor (Bellusi, 2017; Wylie, 2010).

247
00:35:31,877 --> 00:35:34,602
Hydrops is an ominous finding.

248
00:35:34,602 --> 00:35:43,764
If the MCA peak systolic velocity is elevated or hydrops is identified, urgent fetal transfusion or delivery should be considered.

249
00:35:43,764 --> 00:35:50,239
In more than 80 percent of cases, no etiology of the fetomaternal hemorrhage is identified.

250
00:35:50,239 --> 00:36:00,302
One limitation of quantitative tests for fetal cells in the maternal circulation is that they do not provide information regarding the timing or chronicity of hemorrhage .

251
00:36:00,302 --> 00:36:08,289
Anemia that develops gradually, as with alloimmunization, is generally better tolerated by the fetus than acute anemia.

252
00:36:08,289 --> 00:36:14,252
Chronic anemia may not produce fetal heart rate abnormalities until the fetus is moribund.

253
00:36:14,252 --> 00:36:24,339
In contrast, significant acute hemorrhage may result in profound fetal neurological impairment from cerebral hypoperfusion, ischemia, and infarction.

254
00:36:24,339 --> 00:36:30,252
In some cases, fetomaternal hemorrhage is identified during stillbirth evaluation .

255
00:36:30,252 --> 00:36:33,114
# Hemorrhage Quantification.

256
00:36:33,114 --> 00:36:44,964
Estimating the volume of fetomaternal hemorrhage is needed to calculate the appropriate dose of anti-D immune globulin if the woman is D-negative, and it may also influence obstetrical management.

257
00:36:44,964 --> 00:36:54,602
The most commonly used quantitative test for fetal red cells in the maternal circulation is the acid elution or Kleihauer-Betke (KB) test .

258
00:36:54,602 --> 00:37:01,664
Fetal erythrocytes contain hemoglobin F, which is more resistant to acid elution than hemoglobin A.

259
00:37:01,664 --> 00:37:06,339
Following exposure to acid, only fetal hemoglobin remains.

260
00:37:06,339 --> 00:37:13,327
Therefore, after staining, the fetal erythrocytes appear red and adult erythrocytes appear as “ghosts” .

261
00:37:13,327 --> 00:37:18,377
The fetal cells are then counted and expressed as a percentage of adult cells.

262
00:37:18,377 --> 00:37:26,739
The fetal blood volume involved in the fetomaternal hemorrhage may be calculated using the following formula: Figure 18-3.

263
00:37:26,739 --> 00:37:31,389
Kleihauer-Betke test demonstrating massive fetomaternal hemorrhage.

264
00:37:31,389 --> 00:37:42,464
After acidelution treatment, fetal red cells rich in hemoglobin F stain darkly, whereas maternal red cells with only very small amounts of hemoglobin F stain lightly.

265
00:37:42,464 --> 00:37:52,927
For a pregnant woman of normal size who is normotensive and has reached full-term, the maternal blood volume (MBV) may be estimated as 5000 mL.

266
00:37:52,927 --> 00:38:11,552
Thus, in a woman with a hematocrit of 35 percent and whose fetus has a hematocrit of 50 percent, the calculation for a KB test demonstrating staining of 1.7 percent of sample cells is: The fetal-placental blood volume at term approximates 125 mL/kg.

267
00:38:11,552 --> 00:38:17,352
For a 3000-g fetus, that would equate to 375 mL.

268
00:38:17,352 --> 00:38:26,902
Thus, this fetus lost approximately 15 percent (60 ÷ 375 mL) of the fetal-placental volume.

269
00:38:26,902 --> 00:38:37,664
Assuming the hematocrit is 50 percent in a term fetus, this 60 mL of whole blood represents 30 mL of red cells lost into the maternal circulation.

270
00:38:37,664 --> 00:38:39,502
A loss of this

271
00:38:39,652 --> 00:38:49,552
magnitude should be well tolerated hemodynamically but would require two 300-μg doses of anti-D immune globulin to prevent alloimmunization.

272
00:38:49,502 --> 00:39:03,752
A more precise method to estimate the maternal blood volume includes a calculation based on the maternal height, weight, and anticipated physiological maternal blood volume accrual (Table 42-1, p. 732).

273
00:39:03,752 --> 00:39:06,777
The KB test is labor intensive.

274
00:39:06,777 --> 00:39:23,889
There are also two scenarios in which the KB may be inaccurate: (1) maternal hemoglobinopathies in which the fetal hemoglobin level is elevated, such as β-thalassemia, and (2) at or near term, because the fetus may already be producing hemoglobin A.

275
00:39:23,889 --> 00:39:39,714
Another method of quantifying fetomaternal hemorrhage is with flow cytometry, which uses monoclonal antibodies to hemoglobin F or to the D antigen and then measures the degree of fluorescence (Chambers, 2012; Welsh, 2016).

276
00:39:39,714 --> 00:39:46,264
Flow cytometry is an automated test that can analyze a greater number of cells than the KB test.

277
00:39:46,264 --> 00:40:09,077
It is also unaffected by maternal levels of fetal hemoglobin and by fetal levels of hemoglobin A. Flow cytometry has been reported to be more sensitive and accurate than the KB test, however, it uses specialized technology not routinely available in many hospitals (Chambers, 2012; Corcoran, 2014; Fernandes, 2007).

278
00:40:09,077 --> 00:40:11,552
Fetal Thrombocytopenia.

279
00:40:11,552 --> 00:40:14,839
# Alloimmune Thrombocytopenia.

280
00:40:14,839 --> 00:40:21,414
This is also referred to as fetal and neonatal alloimmune thrombocytopenia (FNAIT).

281
00:40:21,414 --> 00:40:39,127
Alloimmune thrombocytopenia (AIT) is the most common cause of severe thrombocytopenia among term newborns, with a frequency of 1 to 2 cases per 1000 births (Kamphuis, 2010; Pacheco, 2013; Risson, 2012).

282
00:40:39,127 --> 00:40:45,989
FNAIT is caused by maternal alloimmunization to paternally inherited fetal platelet antigens.

283
00:40:45,989 --> 00:40:54,577
Maternal antiplatelet antibodies cross the placenta in a manner similar to red cell alloimmunization (p. 352).

284
00:40:54,577 --> 00:41:00,877
Unlike immune thrombocytopenia, the maternal platelet count is normal with FNAIT.

285
00:41:00,877 --> 00:41:08,002
And, unlike anti-D alloimmunization, severe sequelae may affect the initial at-risk pregnancy.

286
00:41:08,002 --> 00:41:25,414
Maternal platelet alloimmunization against human platelet antigen-1a (HPA-1a) accounts for 80 to 90 percent of cases and is associated with the greatest severity (Bussel, 1997; Knight, 2011; Tiller, 2013).

287
00:41:25,414 --> 00:41:34,264
This is followed in order of frequency by alloimmunization against HPA-5b, HPA-1b, and HPA-3a.

288
00:41:34,264 --> 00:41:40,189
Alloimmunization to other antigens accounts for only 1 percent of reported cases.

289
00:41:40,189 --> 00:41:46,764
Approximately 85 percent of non-Hispanic white individuals are HPA-1a positive.

290
00:41:46,764 --> 00:41:52,539
Two percent are homozygous for HPA-1b and at risk for alloimmunization.

291
00:41:52,539 --> 00:42:00,139
However, only 10 percent of these women produce antiplatelet antibodies when carrying an HPA-1a fetus.

292
00:42:00,139 --> 00:42:12,202
Approximately a third of affected fetuses or neonates will develop severe thrombocytopenia, and 10 to 20 percent with severe thrombocytopenia sustain an intracranial hemorrhage (ICH) .

293
00:42:12,202 --> 00:42:25,564
As a result, population-based screening studies have identified one case of FNAIT-associated ICH per 25,000 to 60,000 pregnancies (Kamphuis, 2010; Knight, 2011).

294
00:42:25,564 --> 00:42:29,227
FNAIT has a spectrum of presentation.

295
00:42:29,227 --> 00:42:39,552
Neonatal thrombocytopenia may be an incidental finding, the newborn may manifest petechiae, or the fetus or neonate may develop devastating ICH.

296
00:42:39,552 --> 00:42:50,364
Of 600 pregnancies with FNAIT identified through a large international registry, fetal or neonatal ICH complicated just 7 percent of cases .

297
00:42:50,364 --> 00:42:57,077
Hemorrhage affected the first-born child in 60 percent and occurred before 28 weeks’ gestation in half.

298
00:42:57,077 --> 00:43:04,927
A third of affected children died soon after birth, and 50 percent of survivors had severe neurological disabilities.

299
00:43:04,927 --> 00:43:14,514
Bussel and coworkers (1997) evaluated fetal platelet counts before therapy in 107 fetuses with FNAIT.

300
00:43:14,514 --> 00:43:20,377
Thrombocytopenia severity was predicted by a prior sibling with perinatal ICH.

301
00:43:20,377 --> 00:43:26,027
Fifty percent had an initial platelet count <20,000/μL.

302
00:43:26,027 --> 00:43:37,489
And, among those in whom the initial platelet count was >80,000/μL, it dropped by at least 10,000/μL per week in the absence of therapy.

303
00:43:37,489 --> 00:43:48,014
Diagnosis and Management AIT is typically diagnosed following delivery of a neonate with severe and unexplained thrombocytopenia to a woman whose platelet count is normal.

304
00:43:48,014 --> 00:43:49,889
Rarely, the

305
00:43:50,044 --> 00:43:54,656
diagnosis is ascertained after identifying fetal ICH.

306
00:43:54,606 --> 00:44:04,494
The condition recurs in 70 to 90 percent of subsequent pregnancies, is often severe, and usually develops earlier with each successive pregnancy.

307
00:44:04,494 --> 00:44:11,494
Traditionally, fetal blood sampling was performed to detect fetal thrombocytopenia and to tailor therapy.

308
00:44:11,494 --> 00:44:17,669
Platelets were transfused if the fetal platelet count was <50,000/μL.

309
00:44:17,669 --> 00:44:22,694
The reported rate of procedure-related complications exceeds 10 percent .

310
00:44:22,694 --> 00:44:31,731
For this reason, most favor empirical treatment with IVIG instead (Berkowitz, 2006; Pacheco, 2011).

311
00:44:31,731 --> 00:44:41,494
Therapy may be stratified according to whether a prior affected pregnancy was complicated by perinatal ICH, and if so, at what gestational age .

312
00:44:41,494 --> 00:44:50,781
Pioneering work by Bussel (1996) and Berkowitz (2006) and their colleagues demonstrated the efficacy of such treatment.

313
00:44:50,781 --> 00:45:04,569
In one series of 50 pregnancies with fetal thrombocytopenia secondary to FNAIT, IVIG raised the platelet count by approximately 50,000/μL, and no fetus developed ICH .

314
00:45:04,569 --> 00:45:22,319
Among pregnancies at particularly high risk, which was based on a platelet count <20,000/μL or a sibling with FNAIT-associated ICH, the addition of corticosteroids to IVIG therapy was associated with a rise in platelet count in 80 percent of cases .

315
00:45:22,319 --> 00:45:30,944
However, a systematic review identified no consistent benefit of corticosteroid treatment compared with IVIG therapy alone .

316
00:45:30,944 --> 00:45:35,206
Thus, corticosteroid therapy is somewhat controversial.

317
00:45:35,206 --> 00:45:39,794
Cesarean delivery is generally recommended at or near term.

318
00:45:39,794 --> 00:45:42,281
Table 18-3.

319
00:45:42,281 --> 00:45:50,731
Fetal-Neonatal Alloimmune Thrombocytopenia (FNAIT) Treatment Recommendations # Immune Thrombocytopenia.

320
00:45:50,731 --> 00:46:03,319
Also known as immune or idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP), this autoimmune disorder is characterized by antiplatelet IgG antibodies that attack platelet glycoproteins.

321
00:46:03,319 --> 00:46:08,281
The antibodies may cross the placenta and cause fetal thrombocytopenia.

322
00:46:08,281 --> 00:46:13,856
Maternal ITP is discussed in Chapter 59 (p. 1059).

323
00:46:13,856 --> 00:46:18,631
Fetal thrombocytopenia from maternal ITP is usually mild.

324
00:46:18,631 --> 00:46:26,444
However, neonatal platelet levels may fall rapidly after birth and nadir at 48 to 72 hours of life.

325
00:46:26,444 --> 00:46:37,144
Neither the maternal platelet count, identification of antiplatelet antibodies, nor treatment with corticosteroids effectively predicts fetal or neonatal platelet counts .

326
00:46:37,144 --> 00:46:44,981
Importantly, fetal platelet counts are usually adequate to allow vaginal delivery without an increased risk of ICH.

327
00:46:44,981 --> 00:46:55,469
In a review of more than 400 pregnancies with ITP, there was no case of fetal or neonatal ICH and no infant with any central nervous system abnormality .

328
00:46:55,469 --> 00:46:58,706
Fetal blood sampling is not recommended .

329
00:46:58,706 --> 00:47:03,244
Mode of delivery is based on standard obstetrical indications.

330
00:47:03,244 --> 00:47:05,431
Hydrops Fetalis.

331
00:47:05,431 --> 00:47:10,144
This phrase has its origins in Middle English, Latin, and Greek.

332
00:47:10,144 --> 00:47:17,744
The condition was described by Ballantyne in 1892 as general dropsy, that is, edema, of the fetus .

333
00:47:17,744 --> 00:47:23,656
Hydrops can be a manifestation of severe illness from a wide variety of etiologies .

334
00:47:23,656 --> 00:47:26,206
Table 18-4.

335
00:47:26,206 --> 00:47:39,594
Categories and Etiologies of Nonimmune Hydrops Fetalis Hydrops is diagnosed by identifying two or more fetal effusions— pleural, pericardial, or ascites—or one effusion plus anasarca .

336
00:47:39,594 --> 00:47:46,494
Sonographically measured skin thickness of >5 mm constitutes edema or anasarca.

337
00:47:46,494 --> 00:48:03,219
Placentomegaly is defined as placental thickness ≥4 cm in the second trimester or ≥6 cm in the third trimester (Bellini, 2009; Society for Maternal–Fetal Medicine, 2015b).

338
00:48:03,219 --> 00:48:12,269
As hydrops progresses in severity, anasarca is an invariable feature and is usually accompanied by placentomegaly and hydramnios.

339
00:48:12,269 --> 00:48:14,844
Figure 18-4.

340
00:48:14,844 --> 00:48:18,331
Sonographic findings that define hydrops.

341
00:48:18,331 --> 00:48:29,169
A. This profile of a 23-week fetus with nonimmune hydrops secondary to B19 parvovirus infection depicts scalp edema (arrowheads) and ascites ().

342
00:48:29,169 --> 00:48:38,244
B. This 34-week fetus had hydrops secondary to an arteriovenous malformation in the brain, known as a vein of Galen aneurysm.

343
00:48:38,244 --> 00:48:43,869
In this coronal image, prominent pleural effusions () outline the lungs (L).

344
00:48:43,869 --> 00:48:48,681
Fetal ascites is also present (arrows), as is anasarca.

345
00:48:48,681 --> 00:48:59,594
C. This axial (transverse) image depicts a pericardial effusion (arrows) in a 23-week fetus with hydrops from B19 parvovirus infection.

346
00:48:59,594 --> 00:49:02,394
The degree of cardiomegaly is

347
00:49:02,548 --> 00:49:10,610
impressive, and the ventricular hypertrophy raises concern for myocarditis, which can accompany parvovirus infection.

348
00:49:10,560 --> 00:49:20,223
D. This axial (transverse) image depicts fetal ascites () in a 15-week fetus with hydrops secondary to large cystic hygromas.

349
00:49:20,223 --> 00:49:23,448
Anasarca is also seen (bracket).

350
00:49:23,448 --> 00:49:31,098
If found in association with red cell alloimmunization, hydrops is termed immune, otherwise, it is nonimmune.

351
00:49:31,098 --> 00:49:37,235
Immune and nonimmune hydrops are postulated to share several physiological abnormalities.

352
00:49:37,235 --> 00:49:42,348
The precise pathogenesis remains unknown but is likely multifactorial.

353
00:49:42,348 --> 00:49:45,548
As shown in Figure 18-5.

354
00:49:45,548 --> 00:49:54,548
these include decreased colloid oncotic pressure, increased hydrostatic or central venous pressure, and enhanced vascular permeability.

355
00:49:54,548 --> 00:49:57,185
Figure 18-5.

356
00:49:57,185 --> 00:50:01,835
Proposed pathogenesis of immune and nonimmune hydrops fetalis.

357
00:50:01,835 --> 00:50:06,798
Data from Bellini, 2009; Lockwood, 2009.

358
00:50:06,798 --> 00:50:09,360
# Immune Hydrops.

359
00:50:09,360 --> 00:50:16,048
This condition results from transplacental passage of maternal antibodies that destroy fetal red cells.

360
00:50:16,048 --> 00:50:25,948
The resultant anemia stimulates marrow erythroid hyperplasia and extramedullary hematopoiesis in the spleen and liver (see Fig. 18-5).

361
00:50:25,948 --> 00:50:33,985
The latter likely causes portal hypertension and impaired hepatic protein synthesis, which lowers plasma oncotic pressure .

362
00:50:33,985 --> 00:50:37,848
Fetal anemia may also raise central venous pressure .

363
00:50:37,848 --> 00:50:48,510
Tissue hypoxia from anemia may increase capillary permeability, such that fluid collects in the fetal thorax, abdominal cavity, and/or subcutaneous tissue.

364
00:50:48,510 --> 00:50:58,098
The incidence of immune hydrops has decreased dramatically with the advent of anti-D immune globulin and with use of MCA Doppler to aid anemia detection.

365
00:50:58,098 --> 00:51:01,873
Only severe anemia results in immune hydrops.

366
00:51:01,873 --> 00:51:14,123
Mari and colleagues (2000) reviewed 70 pregnancies with fetal anemia from red cell alloimmunization and found that all with immune hydrops had hemoglobin values <5 g/dL.

367
00:51:14,123 --> 00:51:20,823
As discussed on page 356, immediate fetal blood transfusion may be lifesaving.

368
00:51:20,823 --> 00:51:23,548
# Nonimmune Hydrops.

369
00:51:23,548 --> 00:51:27,723
At least 90 percent of cases of hydrops are nonimmune .

370
00:51:27,723 --> 00:51:33,935
The prevalence approximates 1 case per 1500 second-trimester pregnancies .

371
00:51:33,935 --> 00:51:39,260
The number of specific disorders that can lead to nonimmune hydrops is extensive.

372
00:51:39,260 --> 00:51:50,560
Etiologies and the proportion of births within each hydrops category from a review of more than 6700 affected pregnancies are summarized in Table 18-4 .

373
00:51:50,560 --> 00:52:02,673
A cause is identified in approximately 60 percent prenatally and in up to 80 percent postnatally (Bellini, 2009, 2015; Santo, 2011).

374
00:52:02,673 --> 00:52:23,110
Of prenatally diagnosed cases, aneuploidy accounts for approximately 20 percent, cardiovascular abnormalities for 15 percent, and infections for 14 percent— the most common of which is parvovirus B19 (Santo, 2011; Sileo, 2020; Sparks, 2019).

375
00:52:23,110 --> 00:52:29,098
In multifetal gestations, twin-twin transfusion syndrome is the most frequent cause .

376
00:52:29,098 --> 00:52:33,473
Fetal deaths and stillbirths are common with nonimmune hydrops.

377
00:52:33,473 --> 00:52:46,723
Overall, only 40 percent of affected pregnancies result in a liveborn neonate, and of these, the neonatal survival rate is just 50 percent (Nassr, 2018; Yeom, 2015).

378
00:52:46,723 --> 00:52:53,810
Importantly, the etiology of nonimmune hydrops varies according to the gestational age at diagnosis.

379
00:52:53,810 --> 00:53:02,973
In a review of 63 pregnancies undergoing genetic testing for hydrops in the first trimester, aneuploidy was the cause in 70 percent .

380
00:53:02,973 --> 00:53:11,348
Of cases diagnosed from 14 through 24 weeks, aneuploidy and congenital infection each accounted for 20 percent.

381
00:53:11,348 --> 00:53:23,760
When nonimmune hydrops presents before 24 weeks’ gestation, the most frequent aneuploidy is 45,X—Turner syndrome, and in such cases, the survival rate is <5 percent .

382
00:53:23,760 --> 00:53:31,860
Recent advances in genetic testing have improved the understanding of hydrops cases that were previously considered idiopathic.

383
00:53:31,860 --> 00:53:45,548
In a review from the University of California Fetal-Maternal Consortium, whole exome sequencing was studied in 127 cases of unexplained nonimmune hydrops in which traditional genetic testing was uninformative .

384
00:53:45,548 --> 00:53:49,648
Nearly 30 percent had a pathogenic genetic variant.

385
00:53:49,648 --> 00:53:56,160
The most common etiology was RASopathies, and many such cases resulted in Noonan syndrome.

386
00:53:56,160 --> 00:54:03,810
It is anticipated that as experience with whole exome sequencing accrues, the diagnostic yield will continue to increase.

387
00:54:03,810 --> 00:54:05,410
Although the

388
00:54:05,572 --> 00:54:11,197
prognosis of nonimmune hydrops is guarded, it depends heavily on etiology.

389
00:54:11,147 --> 00:54:18,709
In a large series from Thailand and Southern China, α0thalassemia is the main cause of nonimmune hydrops.

390
00:54:18,709 --> 00:54:31,422
It accounts for 30 to 50 percent of cases and confers an extremely poor prognosis (Liao, 2007; Ratanasiri, 2009; Suwanrath-Kengpol, 2005).

391
00:54:31,422 --> 00:54:46,184
In contrast, treatable etiologies such as parvovirus infection, chylothorax, and tachyarrhythmias, which each constituting approximately 10 percent of cases, can result in survival in two thirds of cases with fetal therapy .

392
00:54:46,184 --> 00:54:53,734
Diagnostic Evaluation Hydrops is readily detected with prenatal sonography (see Fig. 18-5).

393
00:54:53,734 --> 00:55:06,697
Imaging and laboratory evaluation may identify fetal structural abnormalities, arrhythmias, anemia, aneuploidy, placental abnormalities, and complications of monochorionic twinning.

394
00:55:06,697 --> 00:55:16,197
Depending on the circumstances, initial evaluation includes the following: 1. Indirect Coombs test to identify alloimmunization 2.

395
00:55:16,197 --> 00:55:36,459
Detailed ultrasound examination of the fetus and placenta that includes: • A detailed anatomical survey to assess for the structural abnormalities listed in Table 18-4 • Fetal echocardiography to further evaluate cardiac structure and function • MCA Doppler peak systolic velocity to assess for fetal anemia 3.

396
00:55:36,459 --> 00:55:50,797
Amniocentesis to obtain samples for chromosomal microarray analysis or karyotyping and for parvovirus B19, cytomegalovirus, and toxoplasmosis testing, as discussed in Chapter 67 4.

397
00:55:50,797 --> 00:55:59,022
Kleihauer-Betke test to detect fetomaternal hemorrhage if anemia is suspected, depending on findings and test results 5.

398
00:55:59,022 --> 00:56:04,797
Consideration of testing for alpha-thalassemia and/or inborn errors of metabolism.

399
00:56:04,797 --> 00:56:13,434
Whole exome sequencing has significant potential to identify a genetic etiology if the aforementioned evaluation is not informative .

400
00:56:13,434 --> 00:56:20,547
Counseling should include anticipated turnaround times, costs, and variants of uncertain significance.

401
00:56:20,547 --> 00:56:23,597
It is not recommended for routine use .

402
00:56:23,597 --> 00:56:36,372
In addition, because sequencing is generally performed on the fetus and parents, a parent may be identified or suspected to have an unrelated but medically actionable finding, such as a cancer predisposition.

403
00:56:36,372 --> 00:56:39,159
Isolated Effusion or Edema.

404
00:56:39,159 --> 00:56:44,472
The initial presentation of hydrops may be as a single effusion or anasarca.

405
00:56:44,472 --> 00:56:52,547
Although neither is diagnostic of hydrops, the above evaluation should be considered, and frequent surveillance may be prudent.

406
00:56:52,547 --> 00:56:59,459
A pericardial effusion may precede development of hydrops in fetal parvovirus B19 infection .

407
00:56:59,459 --> 00:57:11,197
Similarly, isolated ascites may be the initial finding in fetal parvovirus B19 infection or may result from a gastrointestinal abnormality such as meconium peritonitis.

408
00:57:11,197 --> 00:57:22,209
An isolated pleural effusion may represent a chylothorax, which is amenable to prenatal diagnosis, and for which fetal therapy may be lifesaving if hydrops develops .

409
00:57:22,209 --> 00:57:34,359
Last, isolated edema, particularly involving the upper torso or the dorsum of the hands and feet, may be found in Turner or Noonan syndrome or may represent congenital lymphedema syndrome .

410
00:57:34,359 --> 00:57:36,747
# Mirror Syndrome.

411
00:57:36,747 --> 00:57:45,897
The association between fetal hydrops and the development of maternal edema, in which the mother mirrors the fetus, is attributed to Ballantyne.

412
00:57:45,897 --> 00:57:52,197
He called the condition triple edema because the fetus, mother, and placenta all became edematous.

413
00:57:52,197 --> 00:57:57,984
Mirror syndrome has been reported to complicate at least 20 percent of hydrops cases .

414
00:57:57,984 --> 00:58:03,134
The etiology of the hydrops is not related to development of mirror syndrome.

415
00:58:03,134 --> 00:58:25,184
It has been associated with hydrops from D alloimmunization, twin-twin transfusion syndrome, placental chorioangioma, fetal cystic hygroma, Ebstein anomaly, sacrococcygeal teratoma, chylothorax, bladder outlet obstruction, supraventricular tachycardia, vein of Galen aneurysm, and various congenital infections .

416
00:58:25,184 --> 00:58:45,059
In a review of more than 50 cases of mirror syndrome, Braun (2010) found that approximately 90 percent of women had edema, 60 percent had hypertension, 40 percent had proteinuria, 20 percent had liver enzyme elevation, and nearly 15 percent had headache and visual disturbances.

417
00:58:45,059 --> 00:58:50,334
Based on these findings, it is reasonable to consider mirror syndrome a form of

418
00:58:50,500 --> 00:58:56,575
severe preeclampsia (Espinoza, 2006; Midgley, 2000).

419
00:58:56,525 --> 00:59:08,200
Others, however, have suggested that it is a separate disease process with hemodilution rather than hemoconcentration (Carbillon, 1997; Livingston, 2007).

420
00:59:08,200 --> 00:59:24,162
Some reports describe the same imbalance of angiogenic and antiangiogenic factors that is observed with preeclampsia, and this suggests a common pathophysiology (Goa, 2013; Hobson, 2020; Llurba, 2012).

421
00:59:24,162 --> 00:59:47,825
These findings, which include elevated concentrations of soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase 1 (sFlt-1), decreased placental growth factor (PlGF) levels, and elevation of soluble vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 1 (sVEGFR1) concentrations, are discussed further in Chapter 40 (p. 694).

422
00:59:47,825 --> 00:59:56,212
In most cases with mirror syndrome, prompt delivery is indicated and followed by resolution of maternal edema and other findings .

423
00:59:56,212 --> 01:00:18,687
However, in isolated cases of fetal anemia, supraventricular tachycardia, hydrothorax, or bladder outlet obstruction, successful fetal treatment has resulted in resolution of both fetal hydrops and maternal mirror syndrome (Goa, 2013; Livingston, 2007; Llurba, 2012; Midgley, 2000).

424
01:00:18,687 --> 01:00:27,237
Normalization of the angiogenic imbalance has also been described following fetal transfusion for parvovirus B19 infection.

425
01:00:27,237 --> 01:00:31,587
Fetal therapy for these conditions is reviewed in Chapter 19.

426
01:00:31,587 --> 01:00:39,537
Given the parallels to severe preeclampsia, delaying delivery to effect fetal therapy should be considered only with caution.

427
01:00:39,537 --> 01:00:44,000
If the maternal condition deteriorates, delivery is recommended.

